      "key": "step-q17269",
      "question": {
        "dom_id": "step-q17269",
        "name": "Question 11",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14225098/assets/7341b6d76c74c20596b627167ee90bed0f00cc435b3d1a23c7b9976e353f79d5.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the curve shown above?",
        "worked_solution": "The diagram shows a circle of radius $|a|=|-3|=3$ centered at the point $(-3,0).$ \n\nIn polar coordinates, the equation of such a circle is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
