      "solution_binding": "43dd1652599bb128d8bc79078553e34653cfa658e56c9c5ac5420806b823d1a8",
      "accepted": true,
      "feedback": {
        "dom_id": "question-281737",
        "name": null,
        "problem": "If the polar curve $r=k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(-3,-3),$  then $k=${{field-1}}.",
        "worked_solution": "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(3\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =k(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\ k & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14225098/assets/72cb94d6ed11f7502e7810d7d201ba71e594ed97a7a3eadd789732ca3dcb8968.png)",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
